6. Trust as Economic Infrastructure

Criminal markets operating without conventional legal enforcement require alternative mechanisms for establishing trust. Research on illicit darknet fentanyl markets has examined identity signals, marketing signals, security behaviour, reviews, and direct expressions of trust, developing a Vendor Trustworthiness Index.

This suggests that trust should be modelled not merely as a property of an individual vendor but as an ecosystem-level infrastructure:

T = f(\text{Reviews}, \text{Reputation}, \text{Identity signals}, \text{Security behavior}, \text{Transaction history}, \text{Network position})

The temporal derivative:

\Delta T(t)

may contain information about ecosystem instability. A sudden divergence between reputation indicators and transaction activity could indicate market migration, reputation manipulation, exit-scam dynamics, platform fragmentation, or the emergence of competing markets. These are hypotheses to be experimentally validated rather than assumed.

6.1 Operationalizing Trust

Dimension Proxy Indicator Data Source
Reviews Volume, sentiment, temporal clustering Market scraping, OSINT
Reputation Account age, feedback scores, dispute rates Market data, forum analysis
Identity signals PGP key continuity, cross-platform pseudonym linkage Blockchain, OSINT
Security behavior 2FA adoption, escrow usage, multisig patterns Blockchain, market features
Transaction history Wallet age, transaction frequency, counterparty diversity Blockchain analysis
Network position Betweenness centrality, multihomer status Graph analysis

These proxies are imperfect and subject to adversarial manipulation. DHO therefore treats T as a probabilistic estimate with explicit uncertainty quantification, not a definitive measurement.

