7. Multimodal Graph Intelligence

7.1 Formal Schema

The DHO proposes a multilayer graph rather than a single transaction graph.

Entity layer:

V_E = \{ \text{pseudonyms, markets, wallets, domains, PGP identifiers, public artifacts} \}

Relationship layer:

E_R = \{ \text{co-occurrence, transaction, referral, public association, temporal correlation} \}

Temporal layer:

G(t_1), G(t_2), \ldots, G(t_n)

Financial layer (where legally available):

G_F = (V_F, E_F)

representing public blockchain transactions.

Semantic layer: NLP produces:

X_{NLP} = \{ \text{entities, categories, relationships, semantic signals} \}

The complete system is:

\boxed{ G^* = G_E \oplus G_R \oplus G_T \oplus G_F \oplus G_{NLP} }

7.2 Alignment with Emerging Infrastructure

The World Economic Forum's Cosmos initiative represents the first open-source knowledge graph of the cybercrime ecosystem, creating a universal ontology that maps, classifies, and connects components including threat actors, infrastructure, criminal services, marketplaces, intermediaries, and monetization chains. Cosmos is designed as an interactive knowledge graph revealing connections between threat actors, infrastructure, tools, and financial enablers, with the underlying open-source formal ontology available for community extension. DHO is designed to be interoperable with Cosmos, adopting its ontology while extending it to darknet-market-specific entities and harm measurement.

7.3 Provenance Requirement

Every extracted fact must carry:

\text{Evidence} = (\text{Source}, \text{Timestamp}, \text{Extraction Method}, \text{Confidence})

This provenance requirement is enforced at the schema level and is non-negotiable. To ensure legal defensibility and auditability, provenance records are stored in an immutable, append-only cryptographic audit log using a Merkle-tree-backed data lineage store. Formally, let \mathcal{L} be the append-only log. Each entry e_i is hashed:

h_i = H(e_i \| h_{i-1})

where H is a collision-resistant hash function. The Merkle root R_t at time t provides a succinct commitment to the entire log history, enabling efficient verification without revealing underlying data.

7.4 Privacy-Preserving Feature Engineering

A critical design constraint is that privacy-enhancing tool usage itself must not be used as a positive risk feature. The multimodal graph engine must avoid treating the following as risk indicators: use of Tor, I2P, or other anonymity networks; use of encrypted messaging; use of privacy coins; use of coinjoin or mixing services; use of VPNs or proxy chains.

Risk signals (R_F) must rely strictly on behavioral coordination and transactional interaction topologies with known illicit artifacts, not on the mere employment of privacy technology. Formally, let \mathcal{P}_{\text{priv}} be the set of privacy-enhancing features. The risk model must satisfy:

\frac{\partial R}{\partial \mathcal{P}_{\text{priv}}} = 0

This constraint is enforced through adversarial debiasing during model training and through post-hoc feature attribution audits.

