9. Financial and Blockchain Intelligence

Public blockchains provide an unusual research opportunity. Unlike conventional financial systems, many blockchain transaction histories are permanently observable.

9.1 The Elliptic2 Benchmark

The Elliptic2 dataset contains 122,000 labeled subgraphs of Bitcoin clusters within a background graph consisting of 49 million node clusters and 196 million edge transactions. Each node has 43 features and each edge 95 features. The dataset provides subgraphs known to be linked to illicit activity for learning the set of "shapes" that money laundering exhibits in cryptocurrency. The important principle for DHO is:

\boxed{ \text{Analyze observable transaction structure} }

rather than:

\boxed{ \text{Construct mass personal surveillance} }

9.2 Structural Risk Signal

The financial layer generates structural risk signals:

R_F = f(\text{graph topology}, \text{temporal patterns}, \text{known labeled structures}, \text{cross-entity relationships})

The output remains probabilistic:

R_F(x) > \tau \quad \text{means: candidate requiring authorized review}

not: criminal.

9.3 Deanonymization Risk and Mitigation

Blockchain analysis carries inherent deanonymization risk. Even "public" transaction data can be linked to individuals through exchange KYC records, IP address correlation, timing analysis, and amount fingerprinting. DHO mitigates this risk through aggregation (analysis focuses on structural patterns, not individual transactions), differential privacy (noise injection prevents individual identification), access controls (raw transaction data is accessible only to authorized analysts under audit), purpose limitation (data is used only for ecosystem-level harm assessment), and retention limits (data is deleted after the minimum period necessary for research objectives).

