10. The Criminal Ecosystem Dependency Graph (CEDG)

10.1 Heterogeneous Multi-Layer Graph Architecture

Let the Criminal Ecosystem Dependency Graph at time t \in \mathbb{R}^+ be represented as a time-varying, weighted, heterogeneous multi-layer directed graph:

\mathcal{G}(t) = \left( \mathcal{V}(t), \mathcal{E}(t), \mathcal{L}, \mathcal{W}(t) \right)

Node Set \mathcal{V}(t)

The node set is partitioned into distinct functional entity types across layers \mathcal{L}_{\text{node}}:

\mathcal{V}(t) = \bigcup_{k \in \mathcal{L}_{\text{node}}} \mathcal{V}_k(t)

\mathcal{L}_{\text{node}} = \{ \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{V}_{\text{vend}}, \mathcal{B}, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{P}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{L}_{\text{log}}, \mathcal{F} \}

where \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{M}}: Marketplaces; \mathcal{V}_{\text{vend}}: Vendors/Sellers; \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{B}}: Buyers/Customers; \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{I}}: Infrastructure Nodes; \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{C}}: Encrypted Communication Channels; \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{P}}: Payment Systems; \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{R}}: Reputation Indexers & Review Aggregators; \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{L}_{\text{log}}}: Logistics & Drop Networks; \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{F}}: Financial Off-Ramps & Laundering Clusters.

Edge Set \mathcal{E}(t) and Multi-Layer Tensor \mathcal{W}(t)

An edge e_{ij}^{(l)}(t) = (v_i, v_j, l, t) \in \mathcal{E}(t) denotes a directed dependency or interaction of layer type l \in \mathcal{L}_{\text{edge}} from upstream capability v_i to downstream consumer v_j. The coupling weights are encoded in a rank-3 dynamic Adjacency Tensor:

\mathcal{A}_{ij, l}(t) = w_{ij}^{(l)}(t) \cdot \mathbb{I}\left( e_{ij}^{(l)}(t) \in \mathcal{E}(t) \right)

where w_{ij}^{(l)}(t) \in [0, 1] parameterizes the structural dependency strength or volume flow between node v_i and node v_j under relation l.

10.2 Node Capability & Ecosystem Viability Operators

10.2.1 Upstream-Downstream Dependency Operator

Each entity v_j \in \mathcal{V}(t) requires a non-zero aggregate input from its critical upstream dependencies to remain operational. Let \mathcal{N}_{\text{in}}^{(l)}(v_j, t) denote the in-neighbors of v_j under edge layer l. The Functional Operational State \psi_j(t) \in [0, 1] of node v_j is governed by a soft-threshold activation function over its incoming dependency weights:

\psi_j(t) = \sigma\left( \sum_{l \in \mathcal{L}_{\text{edge}}} \beta_l S_{jl}(t) - \theta_j \right) \cdot \prod_{k \in \mathcal{D}_j^{\text{crit}}} \mathbb{I}(S_{jk}(t) > 0)

where S_{jl}(t) = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{N}_{\text{in}}^{(l)}(v_j, t)} w_{ij}^{(l)}(t)\psi_i(t), \mathcal{D}_j^{\text{crit}} \subset \mathcal{L}_{\text{edge}} is the set of non-substitutable, critical dependency layers for node v_j, \sigma(x) = (1 + e^{-x})^{-1} is the logistic activation function, and \theta_j is the operational vulnerability threshold of node v_j.

10.2.2 Global Ecosystem Viability Index \Phi(\mathcal{G}(t))

The aggregate functional viability of the criminal ecosystem is modeled via the spectral radius of its coupled supra-adjacency matrix \mathbf{M}(t) and cross-layer flow conservation:

\Phi(\mathcal{G}(t)) = \rho(\mathbf{M}(t)) \cdot \left( \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{vend}}|} \sum_{v_j \in \mathcal{V}_{\text{vend}}} \psi_j(t) \right) \cdot \log \left( 1 + \mathcal{V}_{\text{liq}}(t) \right)

where \mathcal{V}_{\text{liq}}(t) is the Liquidity Transfer Velocity defined across the financial layer \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{F}}:

\mathcal{V}_{\text{liq}}(t) = \iint_{\Omega} \left\| \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\mathcal{F}}(x, t)}{\partial t} \right\|_1 dx

This formulation captures the intuition that ecosystem viability depends not only on the number of active nodes but also on their functional integration and the velocity of financial flows. The spectral radius is normalized by the number of nodes to ensure scale-invariance.

10.3 Intervention and Adversarial Response Operators

Let u(t) \in \mathcal{U} be the Law Enforcement Intervention Vector applied at time t, and let a(t) \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{adv}} be the Adversarial Adaptation Vector deployed by the criminal ecosystem.

10.3.1 Intervention Operator \mathcal{I}_{u(t)}

An intervention u(t) = \left( \mathbf{u}_{\text{seize}}, \mathbf{u}_{\text{block}}, \mathbf{u}_{\text{disrupt}}, \mathbf{u}_{\text{payment}}, \mathbf{u}_{\text{reputation}}, \mathbf{u}_{\text{logistics}} \right) acts directly on graph topology by removing nodes, degrading edge weights, or freezing financial flows:

\mathcal{I}_{u(t)} : \mathcal{G}(t) \mapsto \mathcal{G}^{\dagger}(t)

\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(t) = \mathcal{V}(t) \setminus \{ v_i \in \mathcal{V}(t) \mid u_{\text{seize}, i}(t) = 1 \}

w_{ij}^{(l)\dagger}(t) = w_{ij}^{(l)}(t) \cdot \left( 1 - u_{\text{disrupt}, ij}^{(l)}(t) \right)

10.3.2 Adversarial Adaptation Operator \mathcal{A}_{a(t)}

Following an intervention at time t, the ecosystem undergoes self-reorganization driven by decentralization, multihomer rerouting, and platform switching. The strategy a^*(t) adopted by the collective threat actors is modeled as the solution to a constrained utility maximization problem:

a^*(t) = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{adv}}} \mathbb{E}_{\epsilon_t} \left[ \sum_{k=1}^{K} \gamma^k \Phi\left( \mathcal{G}(t+k) \right) - \mathcal{C}_{\text{adapt}}(a \mid \mathcal{G}^{\dagger}(t)) \right]

subject to operational budget constraints \mathcal{C}_{\text{adapt}}(a) \le B_{\text{adv}}, where \mathcal{C}_{\text{adapt}} encompasses:

· Migration Friction: Cost of deploying replacement infrastructure.
· Reputation Re-establishment Cost: Entropy loss in migrating PGP identity keys and historical trust metrics.
· Financial Laundering Rerouting Fee: Transaction cost multipliers imposed by switching to lower-liquidity, high-anonymity channels.

This game-theoretic formulation captures the co-evolutionary nature of the ecosystem-intervention dynamic: interventions are not exogenous shocks but strategic moves that provoke adaptive responses.

10.4 Dynamic Ecosystem Adaptation Differential Equations

The temporal state transition function governing the ecosystem response F is expressed in continuous-time stochastic differential form:

\mathrm{d}\mathcal{G}(t) = F\left( \mathcal{G}(t), u(t), a^*(t) \right) \mathrm{d}t + \boldsymbol{\Sigma}\left( \mathcal{G}(t) \right) \mathrm{d}\mathbf{W}(t)

Expanding this operator into structural components yields the triad of ecosystem evolution:

\boxed{ \mathcal{G}(t+\Delta t) = F\left( \mathcal{G}(t), u(t), a^*(t), \epsilon(t) \right) = \mathcal{G}(t) \oplus \Delta \mathcal{G}_{\text{birth}} \ominus \Delta \mathcal{G}_{\text{death}} \otimes \Delta \mathcal{G}_{\text{rewire}} }

10.4.1 Node Death Dynamics

\Delta \mathcal{G}_{\text{death}}(t) = \{ v_i \in \mathcal{V}(t) \mid u_{\text{seize}, i}(t) = 1 \lor \psi_i(t) < \eta_{\text{fail}} \}

10.4.2 Node Birth & Migration Dynamics

\lambda_{\text{birth}}(t) = \kappa \cdot \mathcal{D}_{\text{unmet}}(t) \cdot \mu(\mathcal{G}(t))

\mathcal{D}_{\text{unmet}}(t) = \max \left( 0, \, \text{Demand}_0 - \sum_{j \in \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{M}}} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{V}_{\text{vend}}} w_{ij}^{(\text{trade})}(t) \psi_j(t) \right)

where \mu(\mathcal{G}(t)) is the Multihomer Connectivity Factor:

\mu(\mathcal{G}(t)) = \frac{\left| \left\{ v \in \mathcal{V}_{\text{vend}} \mid \deg_{\text{out}}^{(\text{market})}(v) \ge 2 \right\} \right|}{\left| \mathcal{V}_{\text{vend}} \right|}

10.4.3 Edge Rewiring & Routing Adaptation

\mathbb{P}\left( e_{ij}^{(l)} \text{ forms at } t+\Delta t \mid v_i \text{ displaced} \right) = \frac{T_j(t) \cdot \exp\left( -\alpha \cdot d_{\text{graph}}(i, j) \right)}{\sum_{k \in \mathcal{V}_l} T_k(t) \cdot \exp\left( -\alpha \cdot d_{\text{graph}}(i, k) \right)}

10.5 Quantitative Ecosystem Resilience Metric (R_E)

Using the dynamic trajectory under adaptation F, the ecosystem resilience R_E to an intervention policy u over horizon T is formally specified as:

\boxed{ R_E(\mathcal{G}, u) = \int_{t_0}^{t_0 + T} \frac{\Phi\left( F(\mathcal{G}(t), u(t), a^*(t), \epsilon(t)) \right)}{\Phi(\mathcal{G}(t_0))} \, \mathrm{d}t }

Normalized Decomposition of R_E

In discrete operational terms, R_E decomposes into three verifiable network observables:

R_E = w_1 \cdot \mu(\mathcal{G}) + w_2 \cdot \frac{|\mathcal{S}_{\text{S2S}}(\mathcal{G})|}{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{vend}}|} + w_3 \cdot \exp\left( -\lambda_{\text{rec}} \tau_{\text{migration}} \right)

where \mu(\mathcal{G}): Multihomer ratio; \frac{|\mathcal{S}_{\text{S2S}}(\mathcal{G})|}{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{vend}}|}: Relative size of the Seller-to-Seller Giant Component in co-occurrence layers; \tau_{\text{migration}}: Characteristic recovery half-life required for financial flow velocity to regain 1 - \frac{1}{e} of pre-intervention baseline following market closure. The weights w_1, w_2, w_3 are calibrated empirically using historical intervention events and subjected to sensitivity analysis.

