14. Darknet Harm Index (DHI)

The central proposed measurement is not an individual score. It is an ecosystem-level harm-capacity index.

14.1 Formal Definition

\text{DHI} = w_S S + w_E E + w_F F + w_R R + w_L L + w_C C

where all components are normalized to [0,1]:

· S — Scale: \log(\text{listings}) / \log(\text{Hydra peak})
· E — Economic capacity: \text{inflow} / \$1.7\text{B baseline}
· F — Harmful-content / exploitation potential: weighted by severity, with CSAM weighted 10\times following IOCTA prioritization
· R — Resilience: R_E from Section 10.5, normalized to [0,1]
· L — Victim impact: mean Australian harm index score for relevant offence categories, normalized
· C — Structural centrality / trophic level: network-based measure of ecosystem importance, normalized

The structural centrality multiplier C addresses the concern that sophisticated actors may deliberately fragment operations into lower-volume, higher-impact niches. Formally:

C = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \sum_{v \in \mathcal{V}} \text{BC}(v) \cdot \text{TL}(v)

where \text{BC}(v) is the betweenness centrality of node v in the CEDG and \text{TL}(v) is the trophic level of v. Trophic level is defined following ecological network theory: in a directed network, the trophic level allows one to assign a height to each node in such a way that on average the height goes up by one along each edge. For all non-primary nodes, the trophic level is defined recursively as a weighted average of the levels of their resource vertices. Trophic coherence—a measure of how neatly species fall into distinct levels—has been shown to be a better statistical predictor of linear stability than size or complexity in ecological networks. DHO adopts this analogy: ecosystem stability and resilience are partly determined by the coherence of the trophic structure, not merely by node count or transaction volume.

14.2 Weight Calibration

The weights are calibrated empirically and subjected to sensitivity analysis:

\frac{\partial \text{DHI}}{\partial w_i}

must be reported to determine whether conclusions are robust to weighting choices.

Calibration methodology:

1. Expert elicitation: Structured interviews with criminologists, law enforcement, and victim advocates to derive prior weights.
2. Multi-criteria decision analysis (MCDA): Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) or TOPSIS to aggregate expert judgments.
3. Historical validation: Regress DHI against observed victim harm in historical datasets.
4. Sensitivity analysis: Monte Carlo simulation to assess DHI robustness under weight perturbation.

Worked example: Suppose expert elicitation yields prior weights w_S = 0.15, w_E = 0.20, w_F = 0.25, w_R = 0.10, w_L = 0.15, w_C = 0.15, summing to 1.0. For a hypothetical ecosystem with normalized components S = 0.6, E = 0.7, F = 0.4, R = 0.5, L = 0.6, C = 0.8, the DHI is 0.15(0.6) + 0.20(0.7) + 0.25(0.4) + 0.10(0.5) + 0.15(0.6) + 0.15(0.8) = 0.09 + 0.14 + 0.10 + 0.05 + 0.09 + 0.12 = 0.59. Sensitivity analysis would then vary each weight by \pm 0.05 to determine whether the ranking of this ecosystem relative to others is stable.

14.3 Uncertainty Quantification

DHI is reported with uncertainty intervals:

\text{DHI} \pm \Delta \text{DHI}

where \Delta \text{DHI} is derived from input data uncertainty (e.g., scraping coverage, blockchain clustering errors), model uncertainty (e.g., graph neural network variance), and weight uncertainty (e.g., expert disagreement).

14.4 Fundamental Constraint

\boxed{ \text{DHI applies to ecosystems, not people.} }

DHI is not a risk score for individuals. It is a measure of the harm-generation capacity of an ecosystem. Any use of DHI for individual targeting would violate the core ethical premise of DHO.

