16. Privacy-Preserving Collaborative Intelligence

Some of the most valuable information relevant to financial crime exists across institutional boundaries. Banks, exchanges, cybersecurity organizations, researchers, and law-enforcement bodies may each possess only a fragment of the relevant evidence.

16.1 Federated Architecture

DHO investigates federated intelligence where D_1, D_2, \ldots, D_n remain locally controlled, while institutions exchange constrained model information:

M_1, M_2, \ldots, M_n \rightarrow M^*

rather than:

D_1 + D_2 + \cdots + D_n \rightarrow \text{Central Surveillance Database}

16.2 Technical Stack

Privacy-preserving techniques include federated learning with secure aggregation, differential privacy with formal (\varepsilon, \delta)-guarantees, secure multiparty computation (SMPC), and homomorphic encryption. Recent research has demonstrated privacy-preserving federated graph learning frameworks that enable collaborative anti-money laundering without exposing raw user data, employing a Boundary Embedding Exchange protocol that shares only compressed, non-invertible graph neural network representations of boundary accounts.

16.3 Federated Graph Neural Networks

Standard federated learning struggles with non-IID graph topologies. DHO therefore investigates federated subgraph matching, where each institution computes embeddings for its local subgraph and a secure aggregation protocol combines embeddings without revealing raw structure. Formally, let \mathcal{G}_i = (\mathcal{V}_i, \mathcal{E}_i) be the local graph at institution i. The federated objective is:

\min_\theta \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathcal{L}_i(\theta) + \lambda \mathcal{R}(\theta)

where \mathcal{L}_i is the local loss, \mathcal{R} is a regularization term, and \theta are the shared model parameters. Updates are aggregated using secure aggregation:

\theta^{(t+1)} = \theta^{(t)} - \frac{\eta}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \text{SecAgg}\left( \nabla \mathcal{L}_i(\theta^{(t)}) \right)

16.4 Privacy Guarantees

DHO requires formal privacy guarantees. Differential privacy provides: for any two neighboring datasets D, D' differing in one record, and any output set S:

P(\mathcal{M}(D) \in S) \le e^\varepsilon P(\mathcal{M}(D') \in S) + \delta

where \varepsilon is the privacy budget and \delta is the failure probability. Secure aggregation ensures that no party learns anything beyond the sum of updates, even if some parties are malicious. DHO assumes an honest-but-curious adversary: parties follow the protocol but may attempt to infer information from received messages. Protection against malicious adversaries requires additional mechanisms such as zero-knowledge proofs.

16.5 Failure Modes and Mitigations

