21. Threat Model

DHO assumes an adaptive adversary that may change marketplaces, manipulate reputation, fragment infrastructure, change payment pathways, migrate between platforms, create synthetic identities, generate misleading public information, exploit weaknesses in analytical models, poison training data, and perform model inversion or evasion attacks. Therefore:

\text{Adversary}_{t+1} \neq \text{Adversary}_t

The system must continuously measure concept drift:

P_t(X, Y) \neq P_{t+1}(X, Y)

and detect when previously valid models cease to generalize.

