23.2 Dual-Key Human Oversight Protocol

To ensure that AI risk signals never bypass due process, DHO implements a mandatory dual-key validation requirement for investigative leads. The signal is independently verified by two qualified human analysts from distinct institutional tiers: an Operational Analyst who assesses technical validity and investigative significance, and a Constitutional Privacy Compliance Officer who assesses legal compliance, privacy impact, and proportionality. Both must approve before an official human-led inquiry can be initiated. Formally:

\text{Lead} = \begin{cases}
\text{Approved} & \text{if } \mathcal{R}_i > \tau \land \text{Approve}_{\text{op}} \land \text{Approve}_{\text{priv}} \\
\text{Rejected} & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

This protocol aligns with the EU AI Act's requirement for separate verification by at least two natural persons for high-risk AI systems, subject to proportionality considerations in law enforcement contexts.

23.3 Algorithmic Red-Teaming and Bias Audits

Before deploying offline prototypes against benchmark datasets, institutional governance mandates independent red-teaming to test whether the system exhibits disparate impact against marginalized geopolitical regions where legitimate populations rely on privacy infrastructure due to state surveillance or financial exclusion.

Red-teaming objectives:

1. Evasion: Can adversaries evade detection through synthetic multihomer injection, reputation-score poisoning, or privacy-tool decoys?
2. False positives: Are legitimate actors disproportionately flagged? Metrics: legitimate-actor burden (LAB) and investigation cost per true positive.
3. Bias: Does the system exhibit disparate impact across regions, demographics, or use cases? Metrics: demographic parity difference, equalized odds difference.
4. Robustness: How does the system perform under adversarial conditions? Metrics: performance degradation under 20% adversarial clients, gradient poisoning resistance.
5. Privacy: Can the system be used to deanonymize individuals? Metrics: membership inference attack success rate, model inversion reconstruction error.

Pass criteria: A red-team exercise is passed only if LAB < 0.05, investigation cost per true positive < threshold, and privacy attack success rates are statistically indistinguishable from random guessing.

