Appendix B: Mathematical Foundations and Proofs

B.1 Trophic Level and Trophic Coherence

Definition B.1.1 (Trophic Level). Let \mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E}) be a directed graph. The trophic level l_i of node i is defined as the solution to the linear system:

l_i = 1 + \frac{1}{k_i^{\text{in}}} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}_{\text{in}}(i)} l_j

for all non-basal nodes (k_i^{\text{in}} > 0), and l_i = 1 for basal nodes (k_i^{\text{in}} = 0).

Definition B.1.2 (Trophic Incoherence). For each edge (i,j), define the trophic distance x_{ij} = l_i - l_j. The trophic incoherence parameter is:

q = \sqrt{\langle x^2 \rangle - 1}

where \langle x^2 \rangle is the second moment of the distribution of x_{ij} over all edges.

Lemma B.1.3. For a maximally coherent network with constant interaction strengths, the trophic incoherence parameter satisfies q = 0, and the network is linearly stable.

Proof sketch. In a maximally coherent network, all edges connect nodes at exactly integer trophic distance x_{ij} = 1. The distribution of x is a Dirac delta at 1, so \langle x^2 \rangle = 1, giving q = 0. Johnson et al. (2014) prove that the leading eigenvalue of the interaction matrix for such a network has negative real part under constant interaction strengths, guaranteeing linear stability.

Proposition B.1.4 (Trophic Coherence and Ecosystem Resilience). In the CEDG, the trophic coherence of the financial layer \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{F}} and the logistics layer \mathcal{L}_{\text{log}} is positively associated with the ecosystem resilience metric R_E.

Justification. Trophic coherence is a better statistical predictor of linear stability than size or complexity in ecological networks. By analogy, criminal ecosystems with coherent financial and logistics flows—where dependencies are organized into clear hierarchical levels—exhibit greater resistance to disruption because shocks propagate through well-defined channels rather than causing cascading failures across randomly connected components.

