B.2 Spectral Radius and Ecosystem Viability

Definition B.2.1 (Supra-Adjacency Matrix). For a multilayer network with L layers and N nodes per layer, the supra-adjacency matrix \mathbf{M} \in \mathbb{R}^{NL \times NL} encodes both intra-layer and inter-layer couplings:

\mathbf{M} = \begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{A}^{(1)} & \mathbf{C}^{(12)} & \cdots & \mathbf{C}^{(1L)} \\
\mathbf{C}^{(21)} & \mathbf{A}^{(2)} & \cdots & \mathbf{C}^{(2L)} \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
\mathbf{C}^{(L1)} & \mathbf{C}^{(L2)} & \cdots & \mathbf{A}^{(L)}
\end{pmatrix}

where \mathbf{A}^{(k)} is the adjacency matrix of layer k and \mathbf{C}^{(k\ell)} encodes inter-layer couplings.

Lemma B.2.2. The spectral radius \rho(\mathbf{M}) of the supra-adjacency matrix determines the tipping point for system-wide dynamic processes such as epidemic spreading and structural resilience in multilayer networks.

Proposition B.2.3 (Normalized Viability Index). The global ecosystem viability index:

\Phi(\mathcal{G}(t)) = \frac{\rho(\mathbf{M}(t))}{|\mathcal{V}(t)|} \cdot \left( \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{vend}}|} \sum_{v_j \in \mathcal{V}_{\text{vend}}} \psi_j(t) \right) \cdot \log \left( 1 + \mathcal{V}_{\text{liq}}(t) \right)

is scale-invariant in the number of nodes.

Proof. The spectral radius \rho(\mathbf{M}(t)) grows linearly with the number of nodes for a fixed average degree. Dividing by |\mathcal{V}(t)| yields a quantity that depends only on the network's structural properties, not its size. The remaining factors are already normalized: \psi_j \in [0,1] and \log(1+\mathcal{V}_{\text{liq}}) \geq 0. Therefore \Phi is invariant under duplication of the entire network. \square

Corollary B.2.4. For two ecosystems of different sizes with identical structural topology and flow dynamics, \Phi produces identical viability scores, enabling cross-ecosystem comparison.

