B.6 Merkle Tree Security Proofs

Definition B.6.1 (Merkle Tree). A Merkle tree is a binary tree where each leaf holds the hash of a data block and each internal node holds:

h_i = H(h_{\text{left}(i)} \| h_{\text{right}(i)})

where H is a collision-resistant hash function.

Definition B.6.2 (Append-Only Log). A log \mathcal{L} = (e_1, e_2, \ldots, e_n) is append-only if entries can only be added, never modified or deleted. Each entry e_i is hashed as:

h_i = H(e_i \| h_{i-1})

with h_0 = H(\text{genesis}).

Theorem B.6.3 (Tamper Detection). If an adversary modifies any entry e_i in the log, the Merkle root R changes with probability 1 - 2^{-\lambda}, where \lambda is the hash output length in bits.

Proof. Suppose e_i \neq e_i'. Then h_i = H(e_i \| h_{i-1}) \neq H(e_i' \| h_{i-1}) unless a hash collision occurs. By collision resistance, this probability is negligible in \lambda. The changed h_i propagates up the tree: each parent hash depends on its children, so the root R changes. Therefore any modification is detectable with probability 1 - 2^{-\lambda}. \square

Theorem B.6.4 (Consistency Proof). For two Merkle trees of sizes m < n, there exists a proof of consistency of size O(\log n) that the tree of size m is a prefix of the tree of size n.

Proof. The consistency proof consists of the nodes along the paths from the root of the size-m tree to the root of the size-n tree, together with the sibling hashes needed to reconstruct both roots. The number of such nodes is O(\log n). Verification recomputes both roots from the provided hashes and checks equality with the published roots. \square

