B.8 Federated Graph Neural Network Convergence

Definition B.8.1 (Federated Objective). For local graphs \mathcal{G}_i = (\mathcal{V}_i, \mathcal{E}_i) at institutions i = 1, \ldots, n:

\min_\theta \sum_{i=1}^n \mathcal{L}_i(\theta) + \lambda \mathcal{R}(\theta)

Theorem B.8.2 (Convergence Rate). Under standard assumptions (bounded gradients, Lipschitz continuity, bounded heterogeneity), the federated GNN with layer-wise aggregation achieves convergence rate:

\frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^T \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\nabla \mathcal{L}(\theta^{(t)})\|^2 \right] \leq O\left(\frac{1}{T}\right)

where T is the number of communication rounds.

Proof sketch. The proof follows standard federated optimization analysis. The layer-wise aggregation mechanism preserves hierarchical structure. Under bounded gradient norms and the Polyak-Łojasiewicz condition, the expected gradient norm decreases as O(1/T). The privacy noise adds a term of order O(\eta d \ln(1/\delta) / (N^2 \varepsilon^2)) to the bound, where N is the number of clients.

Proposition B.8.3 (Secure Aggregation Correctness). The secure aggregation protocol \text{SecAgg} computes:

\text{SecAgg}\left( \{\nabla \mathcal{L}_i(\theta)\}_{i=1}^n \right) = \sum_{i=1}^n \nabla \mathcal{L}_i(\theta)

without revealing any individual \nabla \mathcal{L}_i(\theta).

Justification. Secure aggregation uses cryptographic masking such that individual updates are hidden, but the sum is recoverable. The protocol is correct as long as at least a threshold number of parties participate honestly.

