B.9 Byzantine-Robust Aggregation

Definition B.9.1 (Krum). Given n updates \theta_1, \ldots, \theta_n, Krum selects the update \theta_i that minimizes:

\sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i} \|\theta_i - \theta_j\|^2

where \mathcal{N}_i is the set of n-f-2 nearest neighbors of \theta_i, and f is the number of Byzantine clients.

Definition B.9.2 (Coordinate-Wise Trimmed Mean). For vectors x_1, \ldots, x_n \in \mathbb{R}^d, the trimmed mean is:

[\text{TM}_{f/n}(x_1, \ldots, x_n)]_j = \frac{1}{n-2f} \sum_{x \in \mathcal{X}_j} x

where \mathcal{X}_j is obtained by deleting the f smallest and f largest values of coordinate j.

Theorem B.9.3 (Robustness Bound). Trimmed mean satisfies:

\|\text{TM}_{f/n}(x) - \bar{x}\| \leq \frac{6f}{n-2f}\left(1 + \frac{f}{n-2f}\right) \cdot \max_i \|x_i - \bar{x}\|

for up to f Byzantine clients.

Theorem B.9.4 (Krum Robustness). Krum satisfies:

\|w - \bar{w}\|^2 \leq \frac{\rho \delta}{|\mathcal{H}|} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{H}} \|w_i - \bar{w}\|^2

where \mathcal{H} is the set of honest clients and \rho = 6 + 6\delta/(1-2\delta).

