B.10 Synthetic Difference-in-Differences

Definition B.10.1 (SDID Estimator). The synthetic difference-in-differences estimator is:

\hat{\tau}^{\text{sdid}} = \left( \bar{Y}_{\text{tr,post}}^{\text{sdid}} - \bar{Y}_{\text{tr,pre}}^{\text{sdid}} \right) - \left( \bar{Y}_{\text{co,post}}^{\text{sdid}} - \bar{Y}_{\text{co,pre}}^{\text{sdid}} \right)

where the weighted averages use unit weights \hat{\omega}^{\text{sdid}} and time weights \hat{\lambda}^{\text{sdid}}.

Definition B.10.2 (Weight Estimation). The SDID weights are obtained by solving:

\left(\hat{\tau}^{\text{sdid}}, \hat{\mu}, \hat{\alpha}, \hat{\beta}\right) = \arg\min_{\tau,\mu,\alpha,\beta} \left\{ \sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{t=1}^T (Y_{it} - \mu - \alpha_i - \beta_t - W_{it}\tau)^2 \hat{\omega}_i^{\text{sdid}} \hat{\lambda}_t^{\text{sdid}} \right\}



Proposition B.10.3 (Consistency). Under the parallel trends assumption and correct specification of the factor model, \hat{\tau}^{\text{sdid}} is a consistent estimator of the average treatment effect on the treated.

Justification. Arkhangelsky et al. (2021) establish consistency under regularity conditions including bounded outcomes, non-degenerate weights, and correct specification of the interactive fixed effects structure.

