Appendix G: Detailed Mathematical Derivations

G.1 Derivation of the Soft-Threshold Activation

Starting from the requirement that \psi_j(t) \in [0,1] and that critical dependencies act as gates:

1. Let S_{jl}(t) = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{N}_{\text{in}}^{(l)}(v_j,t)} w_{ij}^{(l)}(t) \psi_i(t) be the aggregate input from layer l.
2. The logistic function \sigma(x) = (1+e^{-x})^{-1} maps \mathbb{R} \to (0,1), providing smooth saturation.
3. The product of indicators \prod_{k \in \mathcal{D}_j^{\text{crit}}} \mathbb{I}(S_{jk}(t) > 0) enforces hard gating: if any critical layer has zero input, the node is non-functional.
4. The threshold \theta_j shifts the activation point: \sigma(\sum_l \beta_l S_{jl} - \theta_j) is near 0 when total input is below threshold, and near 1 when above.

Boundary behavior:

· \lim_{\sum \beta_l S_{jl} \to -\infty} \psi_j = 0
· \lim_{\sum \beta_l S_{jl} \to +\infty} \psi_j = 1
· \psi_j = 0 if any critical S_{jk} = 0

G.2 Derivation of the Adversarial Best Response

The adversarial optimization problem:

a^*(t) = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{adv}}} \mathbb{E}_{\epsilon_t} \left[ \sum_{k=1}^{K} \gamma^k \Phi(\mathcal{G}(t+k)) - \mathcal{C}_{\text{adapt}}(a \mid \mathcal{G}^{\dagger}(t)) \right]

subject to \mathcal{C}_{\text{adapt}}(a) \leq B_{\text{adv}}.

Lagrangian:

\mathcal{L}(a, \lambda) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{k=1}^K \gamma^k \Phi(\mathcal{G}(t+k)) \right] - \mathcal{C}_{\text{adapt}}(a) - \lambda(\mathcal{C}_{\text{adapt}}(a) - B_{\text{adv}})

First-order condition (interior solution):

\frac{\partial}{\partial a} \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{k=1}^K \gamma^k \Phi(\mathcal{G}(t+k)) \right] = (1+\lambda) \frac{\partial \mathcal{C}_{\text{adapt}}}{\partial a}

Interpretation: The adversary equates the marginal benefit of adaptation (discounted future viability) to the marginal cost, weighted by the shadow price of the budget constraint.

G.3 Derivation of Edge Rewiring Probability

The preferential attachment kernel:

\mathbb{P}(e_{ij}^{(l)} \text{ forms} \mid v_i \text{ displaced}) = \frac{T_j(t) \cdot \exp(-\alpha \cdot d_{\text{graph}}(i,j))}{\sum_{k \in \mathcal{V}_l} T_k(t) \cdot \exp(-\alpha \cdot d_{\text{graph}}(i,k))}

Properties:

1. Normalization: \sum_{j \in \mathcal{V}_l} \mathbb{P}(e_{ij}^{(l)}) = 1 by construction.
2. Preferential attachment: Higher trust T_j increases attachment probability.
3. Distance decay: Exponential decay with graph distance d_{\text{graph}}; parameter \alpha > 0 controls locality.
4. Limit behavior:
   · As \alpha \to 0, attachment is proportional to trust alone (global competition).
   · As \alpha \to \infty, attachment is to nearest neighbors only (local rewiring).

